Q: Multer not creating uploads folder when req.files used but creates folder when req.file used A: <p><code>upload.array()</code> and <code>req.files</code> are <strong>not</strong> the cause of the problem. Multer should create/use the same destination for both <code>single()</code> and <code>array()</code> uploads.</p>
<p>The <code>ENOENT</code> error means the <code>uploads</code> directory <strong>doesn't exist</strong> (or the path is incorrect) when Multer tries to save the file.</p>
<p>Use an absolute path and ensure the directory exists before uploading:</p>
<pre><code>const path = require(&quot;path&quot;);
const fs = require(&quot;fs&quot;);

const uploadDir = path.join(__dirname, &quot;uploads&quot;);

if (!fs.existsSync(uploadDir)) {
  fs.mkdirSync(uploadDir, { recursive: true });
}

const upload = multer({ dest: uploadDir });
</code></pre>
<p>Also note:</p>
<ul>
<li><p><code>upload.single()</code> → access uploaded file with <code>req.file</code>.</p>
</li>
<li><p><code>upload.array()</code> → access uploaded files with <code>req.files</code> (an array).</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p>If <code>single()</code> appears to work but <code>array()</code> doesn't, the issue is likely elsewhere (such as the field name or working directory), not because of <code>req.files</code> itself.</p>

Q: How to do file operations (create/write/update/delete files) using the Flutter &quot;saf&quot; Package A: <blockquote>
<p><strong>Disclosure:</strong> I maintain the <code>saf</code> package linked below.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>On Android 11+ the OS enforces <em>scoped storage</em>, so you can't open a folder you
don't own by <code>File</code> path. The supported way to get durable read/write access to
a folder the <strong>user picks</strong> is the <strong>Storage Access Framework (SAF)</strong> with a
<em>persisted</em> URI permission.</p>
<p>Minimal, complete example using <a href="https://pub.dev/packages/saf" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>saf</code></a>:</p>
<pre><code>import 'package:saf/saf.dart';

final saf = Saf();

// 1. User picks a folder. persistablePermission defaults to true,
//    so the grant survives app restarts.
final dir = await saf.pickDirectory();
if (dir == null) return; // user cancelled

// 2. Work with URIs (content://…), not filesystem paths.
for (final f in await saf.list(dir.uri)) {
  print('${f.name}  ${f.length} B  ${f.mimeType}');
}

// 3. Read a file's bytes.
final bytes = await saf.readFileBytes(someFile.uri);

// 4. Next launch: reuse the grant instead of prompting again.
final grants = await saf.persistedPermissions(); // each has .uri/.read/.write
</code></pre>
<p>Three things that commonly trip people up:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Persist the permission.</strong> Without <code>takePersistableUriPermission</code>, the grant
dies when the process is killed. <code>saf</code> does this for you (it's the default).</li>
<li><strong>Store the URI, not a path.</strong> Under scoped storage there is no real path —
you get a <code>content://</code> URI; persist and reuse <em>that</em>.</li>
<li><strong><code>Android/data</code> and <code>Android/obb</code> cannot be granted via SAF</strong> on Android 11+
— the system blocks them. That's a platform restriction, not a library bug.</li>
</ol>
<p>Full API and recipes: <a href="https://jvoltci.github.io/saf/" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://jvoltci.github.io/saf/</a></p>
